@BUNDLE@

This is a complete, ENCRYPTED backup of a Nova hub: its databases, its
volumes, the files it needs to come up, and the secrets those were created
with. It was written on @HOST@ at @CREATED_AT@ (UTC).

Without the passphrase it is 64 bytes of header and noise. Nobody at Nova has
a copy; if the operator's recorded copy is gone, so is this bundle's content.
The passphrase that opens it has fingerprint @FINGERPRINT@ (that is a
fingerprint, not a hint — it identifies which passphrase, and reveals none of
it).

TO OPEN IT, on a machine that has python3 or docker:

    tar -xOf @BUNDLE@ restore.sh | sh -s -- @BUNDLE@ ./nova-restored

That probes for a working AES-256-GCM decryptor, proves the one it picks
against a known-answer test with your real passphrase before it reads a byte
of the payload, and then extracts and verifies every member against the
manifest sealed inside. If it can find no decryptor it says exactly what to
install; it never proceeds on a maybe.

If you have only docker, it will use one of: @NEEDS_IMAGES@

RUNNING THE CARRIED SCRIPT IS RUNNING CODE FROM THIS BUNDLE. `restore.sh` and
`nova_restore.py` travel here in cleartext and nothing inside this file can
authenticate them — the only hashes that could are sealed in the payload they
exist to open. They are byte-identical to their copies in the Nova repository,
and the published sha256 of nova_restore.py is:

    @READER_SHA256@

Compare that against the digest published in deploy/README.md in the
repository — out of band, not with a number that travelled in this file — or,
better, restore from a checkout of the repository instead. That is the
verified path; this script is the one that works when there is no checkout
yet, which is the situation this whole bundle exists for.

WHAT IS IN IT: run `nova_restore.py --verify-only` to list every member and
check it, or open MANIFEST.json after extracting. The manifest records what
this bundle does NOT carry, and why, for every excluded item — a restore that
cannot say what it is missing invites you to assume it is missing nothing.
